    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "My apologies, I will keep the language strictly technical for your report. The precipitation of micronutrients at higher pH levels is primarily driven by the formation of insoluble metal hydroxides, such as $\\text{Fe}(\\text{OH})_3$ and $\\text{MnO}_2$, effectively reducing the concentration of the bioavailable ionic forms ($\\text{Fe}^{2+}$, $\\text{Mn}^{2+}$) in the soil solution.\n\nVisually, the deficiency symptoms are distinct and provide clear diagnostic markers for the vineyard manager. Iron deficiency, or chlorosis, typically presents as **interveinal chlorosis** on the youngest leaves—the leaves are yellow, but the veins remain distinctly green. Manganese deficiency often presents similarly but can also affect older leaves, exhibiting a mottled yellowing pattern. Early detection of these chlorotic symptoms is critical, as the vines are essentially starving for these elements despite their presence in the soil.",
      "source": "target",
